yes(437,'C','Sodium hydroxide absorbs the acidic oxide CO₂, while CO does not react in this washing step. With sufficient hydroxide, CO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂CO₃ + H₂O. Passing the mixture over hot CuO would instead consume CO by oxidising it to CO₂.',[RX],question='An experiment produces a mixture of CO₂ and CO. Which treatment removes CO₂ while retaining CO?',options=['Pass it over heated copper(II) oxide','Bubble it through concentrated sulfuric acid','Bubble it through sodium hydroxide solution','Bubble it through water'],audit='Corrected old A to C by independent acid-oxide separation reasoning; described the actual separation rather than implying aqueous washing also dries the gas.',check='carbon-dioxide-wash')
yes(438,'B','Chloride ions give a white silver chloride precipitate with aqueous silver nitrate: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s). Excess ammonia dissolves it by forming the soluble complex [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺.',[RX,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/13432?exam_year=1993&page=6'],question='Which property identifies chloride ions from an ionic chloride in aqueous solution?',options=['They can be decomposed by heat','They react with aqueous AgNO₃ to give a white precipitate soluble in excess ammonia','They explode on contact with dry ammonia gas','They react with concentrated sulfuric acid to give white fumes of chlorine gas'],audit='Matching 1993 question confirms D chlorine gas, not hydrogen chloride. Aqueous testing context explicit; no assertion that every ionic chloride is soluble.',check='silver-chloride-complex')
yes(439,'D','Lead(II) ions and bromide ions form insoluble lead(II) bromide: Pb²⁺ + 2Br⁻ → PbBr₂(s). Potassium and nitrate ions remain in solution as spectator ions. The full reaction is Pb(NO₃)₂ + 2KBr → PbBr₂ + 2KNO₃.',[RX,'https://www.schoolngr.com/classroom/chemistry/13545'],question='Dilute aqueous solutions of lead(II) nitrate and potassium bromide are mixed and a precipitate forms. Which list gives the resulting solid and dissolved spectator species?',options=['PbO(s), Br⁻(aq) and KNO₃','Br₂, NO₂(g) and PbBr₂(s)','PbO(s), K⁺(aq), Br(aq) and NO₂(g)','PbBr₂(s), K⁺(aq) and NO₃⁻(aq)'],audit='Exact matching question independently supplies missing lead. Removed duplicated PbO in C as corroborated; optionD spectator nitrate charge restored. Lists species rather than claiming these are balanced coefficients.',check='lead-bromide-balance')
